// dgq_shots.js: the shot list for "Don't Go Quiet On Me", the one source of truth for the animatic
// (scenes/dgq_animatic.js), the treatment doc and the checkpoint page (tools/build_treatment.mjs reads this file).
//
// Fields
//   id     shot id (H hook, V verse, C chorus, P pre-chorus, B bridge, FC final chorus, E end; x = a transition shot)
//   at     cut time: seconds, or 'L<n>' = the beat at or just before line n's first sung word (see shotTime)
//   sec    song section          title  a few words          what   what we see (the board), present tense
//   ly     lyric treatment: hero (huge serif, left; the chorus/hook look) | big (large, centred) | caption (serif
//          karaoke caption, lower left; verses) | shout (full-frame type) | card (the words ride on a card/tweet)
//          Backing-vocal parentheses are always red-pencil marginalia.
//   trans  how we get INTO this shot      card  the full reference (title · who · date): for the page and fact checks,
//          never on screen (Neel, 3 Oct: "Don't have paper references on screen, maybe have the title in the bottom
//          left corner, but no year or author or org")
//   paper  the paper's title, the only reference shown: small, bottom left     quote  { text, who }: a quoted tweet
//   S      true: a character-performance shot (Seedance base clip, then redrawn in JS; the base is never seen)
//   g      the creature's growth 0..5 across the shot [from, to]. Choruses hold one value each, bigger every chorus: it
//          never grows on screen (Neel, 3 Oct: "bigger each time but not grow on screen")
//   eggs   the extra reference layers, each tied to the lyric (the "joke in every corner"); [check] = being verified
//
// Rule for eggs (Neel, 2 Oct): dense, rewarding a careful viewer, but every reference must make sense with its lyric
// and the flow; nothing shoehorned. Visual humour welcome. Timing and transitions are crucial.

// How each section moves: one world per verse, the camera travelling through it, so the verses flow instead of
// cutting between unrelated boards. Choruses are the fixed motif ("the visit") that the verses keep returning to.
